  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q53-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "53",
    "prompt": "Find the value of $n$ for the first term of the sequence $5000,\\ 1000,\\ 200,\\ldots$ that is less than $1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=7$",
      "worked": "$a=5000$, $r=\\frac15$.\n$$\\begin{align*}a_n&<1\\\\5000\\left(\\frac15\\right)^{n-1}&<1\\\\\\left(\\frac15\\right)^{n-1}&<\\frac1{5000}\\\\n-1&>\\log_{1/5}\\left(\\frac1{5000}\\right)\\\\n&>1+\\log_{1/5}\\left(\\frac1{5000}\\right)\\end{align*}$$\nThe inequality reverses because the logarithm base is between $0$ and $1$. The bound is approximately $6.29$, so the least integer satisfying it is $n=7$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
